/**
 * Approach:
 * - Create an empty list to store the postorder traversal.
 * - Use a recursive helper function to traverse the tree.
 * - Follow the postorder sequence:
 *      1. Traverse the left subtree.
 *      2. Traverse the right subtree.
 *      3. Visit the current node and add its value to the list.
 * - Return the final list containing the postorder traversal.
 *
 * Time Complexity: O(n)
 * - Every node is visited exactly once.
 *
 * Space Complexity: O(h)
 * - h is the height of the tree due to the recursion stack.
 * - Best Case (Balanced Tree): O(log n)
 * - Worst Case (Skewed Tree): O(n)
 */
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> postorderTraversal(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> arr = new ArrayList<>();
        postorder(root, arr);
        return arr;
    }

    public void postorder(TreeNode root, List<Integer> arr) {
        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        postorder(root.left, arr);
        postorder(root.right, arr);
        arr.add(root.val);
    }
}
keep in mind:
Who creates the list? → Main function.(empty list)
Who fills the list? → Helper function.
Why pass arr? → So every recursive call uses the same list instead of creating a new one.
